  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 1150 AM CDT Fri Jul 3 2026\n\nSaturday remains likely to be the peak in the current batch of\nhot, humid weather, with more widespread heat index values from\n105 to 110 anticipated. A Heat Advisory has been issued for most\nof eastern Oklahoma along and north of I-40, as well as west\ncentral Arkansas, running from Noon to 8 pm tomorrow. The Kansas\nborder counties, however, have a low chance of being cooler than\nforecast due to the aforementioned outflow boundary, depending on\nwhere it eventually ends up. This will have to be monitored on\nfuture shifts. The outflow boundary will be a focus for isolated\nthunderstorm development in the afternoon, as well as another\nround of the higher terrain diurnal storms from southeast \nOklahoma to northwest Arkansas. The NBM initialization has been\nadjusted to accommodate these scenarios.\n\nSaturday night into Sunday morning remains likely to feature a\nmuch better chance of an MCS moving into the area, as the upper\nlevel ridge shifts west and a disturbance in the northwest flow\naloft that materializes sparks a complex. A low chance of locally\nsevere wind gusts will exist, particularly across parts of\nnortheast Oklahoma, with a general decreasing trend expected with\nsouthward progression. Given the timing of this expected complex,\nlow temperatures on Sunday morning across much of the area north \nof I-40 are likely to be lower than the NBM initialization. This\ntrend will extend to the high temperatures on Sunday, as well,\nwith the expectation of additional daytime storm development with\nany leftover boundaries in addition to residual cloud cover. \n\nInto early next week, most of the synoptic models and ensemble\nsolutions indicate northwest flow and potential for low-amplitude\ntroughing remaining across at least the eastern part of the\nforecast area through Tuesday. The NBM surface wind projection\nfinally came around to this, with light easterly winds through\nthis period. The NBM high temperatures during this period are \nstill likely too high given all of this, with afternoon\ntemperatures near to maybe even just below normal more reasonable.\nLow level flow becomes more westerly, mid to late week, supporting\na warmup and noticeably above normal temperatures.",
